    comment on column "playon"."main_marts_data"."dim_user".has_ever_churned is $dbt_comment_literal_block$**A plan this user actually paid for reached `canceled` or `expired`.**
**Restricted to paid plans in WP-009.** It previously ran over every row of a user's history, so a lapsed free trial counted as churn — 564 trial rows reach `expired` without a cent ever being charged. Someone who tried the product and never converted has not churned; they never retained, and counting them inflates churn with people who were never customers. Trial-only users are identified by `has_ever_paid = false`.
$dbt_comment_literal_block$;
  
    
    
    comment on column "playon"."main_marts_data"."dim_user".is_currently_subscribed is $dbt_comment_literal_block$**The one "subscribed right now" flag.** Replaced a redundant pair — this and `has_open_paid_spell` agreed on all 8,000 users and could not disagree by construction, since `status = 'active'` only ever occurs on a paid plan. Excludes free trials: trialing is not paying. Order-independent, unlike `latest_status`.
$dbt_comment_literal_block$;
  
    
    
    comment on column "playon"."main_marts_data"."dim_user".latest_change_status is $dbt_comment_literal_block$Status of this user's **most recent subscription change**, via `arg_max` on `valid_from`. Note the name: it is not "the user's status", and cannot be for anyone holding concurrent subscriptions — **509 users have their most recent change on a different subscription than their active one** and are correctly still subscribed.
**Was a bug twice over.** An earlier `max(case when is_current_state then status end)` picked the alphabetically largest status (`max('active','canceled') = 'canceled'`), mislabelling 748 users; and it was called `latest_status`, which invited exactly the misreading the data cannot support. For a yes/no question use `is_currently_subscribed`, which is order-independent.
$dbt_comment_literal_block$;
  
    
    
